-- 1176: Blog post - "A Wi-Fi Chip and a Room Full of Beginners: Our 7 April 2017 IoT Class"
--
--       Second post for WSQ - Internet of Things (IoT) Fundamental for Beginners
--       (TGS-2020504020), after 1038's IoTFlow build log, and the earliest by date.
--       The 7 Apr 2017 class at our old premise at Bukit Timah Shopping Centre,
--       taught by Mr Deepak. Cross-links to the IoTFlow sibling post.
--       Ships already published (status 1); linkedin_urn carries the manual-skip
--       sentinel (back-dated post - standing rule, see the blog-pipeline skill).
--       published_at pinned to 2017-04-07.
--
-- TRAINER: the admin supplied only "Mr Deepak" - no surname asserted, no biography
-- or credentials claimed. Copy is limited to: he taught this class.
--
-- PHOTOGRAPH: single group shot from the class album. 1008px-native Facebook
-- export at 109KB - already under the <150KB target; a 900px q62 re-encode came
-- out LARGER (130KB), so the original was uploaded AS-IS (re-encoding an
-- already-compressed JPEG only destroys quality). Verified HTTP 200 at
-- blog/iot/2017-04-07-iot-class-group-photo.jpg. The title slide visible on the
-- screen behind the class dates the run.
--
-- Every course figure was read off the LIVE course page on 2026-08-29: $750
-- before GST, GST $67.50 (9%), nett $442.50 / $292.50, 2 days / 16 hrs +
-- 2 hr assessment at Woods Square Tower 1 (Woodlands), TSC Internet of Things
-- Application PTP-TEM-3002-1.1, funding valid 20 May 2020 - 19 May 2028,
-- IoT kit used in training but not included in the fee.
--
-- FUNDING HONESTY: WSQ funding for this course began 20 May 2020, so the copy
-- says plainly that the 2017 class carried no funding - today's funded fees are
-- contrasted, not retro-fitted.
--
-- VENUE: Bukit Timah Shopping Centre is written as the old premise (past tense);
-- Woods Square, Woodlands is named as the CURRENT venue.
--
-- Notes for future editors:
--  * apply.php splits statements on ";" at end-of-line - the content HTML is ONE line.
--  * Content is pure ASCII; excerpt uses a REAL em-dash via CHAR() (see 1161);
--    meta_description carries NO em-dash entity (see 1164).
--  * FAQ ships as native <details> accordions per the blog-pipeline rules.
--  * Idempotent: WHERE NOT EXISTS on url_key, INSERT IGNORE on tag links.
--  * SG-only guard via @mms_instance.
--  * hero_image_url is NOT set here - generated on prod post-deploy (kicker "IoT").

SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);


INSERT INTO `mmd_blog_post` (`title`, `url_key`, `excerpt`, `content`, `author`, `status`, `published_at`, `related_skus`, `source_sku`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `meta_keywords`, `linkedin_urn`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) SELECT 'A Wi-Fi Chip and a Room Full of Beginners: Our 7 April 2017 IoT Class', 'iot-class-2017-deepak-bukit-timah', CONCAT('Long before IoT carried WSQ funding, a small class gathered at our old premise in Bukit Timah Shopping Centre to wire sensors to the internet. Our 7 April 2017 Internet of Things class, taught by Mr Deepak ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' and why the little Wi-Fi chip from that room is still on the course outline today.'), '<p>On <strong>7 April 2017</strong>, a class gathered at our old premise at <strong>Bukit Timah Shopping Centre</strong> to spend a day connecting things to the internet. The trainer was <strong>Mr Deepak</strong>, and the subject was one that in 2017 still needed explaining at dinner tables: the <strong>Internet of Things</strong>.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/iot/2017-04-07-iot-class-group-photo.jpg" alt="The 7 April 2017 Internet of Things class posing for a group photograph with trainer Mr Deepak at Bukit Timah Shopping Centre, the IoT title slide on the screen behind them" /> <figcaption>The 7 April 2017 IoT class with Mr Deepak at our old Bukit Timah Shopping Centre premise. The title slide on the screen dates the run. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>This is a look back at what learning IoT in Singapore looked like nearly a decade ago &mdash; before the course carried WSQ accreditation, before "smart nation" was daily vocabulary, and before the skills in that room became something the government would help pay for.</p> <h2>2017: when a $5 chip changed who could learn IoT</h2> <p>The reason a beginner class like this could exist at all was a piece of hardware: the <strong>ESP8266</strong>, a Wi-Fi microcontroller that cost a few dollars and could be programmed by anyone with a laptop and a USB cable. Before it, putting a sensor on the internet meant embedded-engineering budgets. After it, a beginner could read a temperature sensor and publish the value to a cloud dashboard in an afternoon.</p> <p>That is exactly what a 2017 IoT class was: wire up a sensor, get the chip on the Wi-Fi, push readings to the cloud, and then &mdash; the part that always got the room excited &mdash; flip the direction and switch something on from a web page.</p> <p>Here is the detail worth smiling at: open <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-internet-of-things-iot-fundamental-for-beginners.html">today''s course outline</a> and the ESP8266 is still there, under "Transmit Data using ESP8266". The platforms around it changed, the funding around it changed, the venue changed &mdash; the little Wi-Fi chip earned its place and kept it.</p> <h2>What the class did that day</h2> <p>The shape of the day in 2017 is the same shape the course has now, because it is the natural shape of the subject:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Understand the moving parts.</strong> Sensors measure, actuators act, and a wireless link carries the data. Once you see IoT as those three pieces, every smart device stops being magic.</li> <li><strong>Collect and post data to the cloud.</strong> Read a sensor with the microcontroller, then transmit the values over Wi-Fi so they land somewhere you can see them from anywhere.</li> <li><strong>Control devices from the cloud.</strong> Reverse the flow: a value or a button on a dashboard reaches back through the internet and switches a real device.</li> <li><strong>Make the data useful.</strong> A single reading is trivia; a chart of readings is information you can act on.</li> </ol> <p>Everyone worked hands-on with the kit. That has never changed either &mdash; IoT is not a subject you can learn by watching someone else''s wires.</p> <h2>What a decade changed &mdash; and what it did not</h2> <ul> <li><strong>The accreditation arrived.</strong> In 2017 this was a short course you simply signed up and paid for. The WSQ version, with funding support, arrived in 2020 &mdash; today the $750 course comes down to <strong>$442.50</strong> or <strong>$292.50</strong> nett for eligible Singaporeans.</li> <li><strong>The stack matured.</strong> MQTT and REST APIs &mdash; the protocols the course teaches for posting and reading data &mdash; went from hobbyist choices to industry standards running smart buildings and factories.</li> <li><strong>The premises changed.</strong> Bukit Timah Shopping Centre was home then; classes now run at Woods Square in Woodlands, five minutes from the MRT.</li> <li><strong>The fundamentals did not move.</strong> Sensors, actuators, a wireless link, a cloud that stores and returns data. Everything Mr Deepak taught in that room is still the backbone of the subject.</li> </ul> <h2>The course today</h2> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-internet-of-things-iot-fundamental-for-beginners.html">WSQ - Internet of Things (IoT) Fundamental for Beginners</a> (course code TGS-2020504020) is a <strong>2-day, 16-hour</strong> course at Beginner level with a 2-hour assessment, held at <strong>Woods Square Tower 1, Woodlands</strong>. It maps to the SkillsFuture Skills Framework TSC <strong>Internet of Things Application (PTP-TEM-3002-1.1)</strong>, with three learning outcomes:</p> <ol> <li>Understand the uses and functions of IoT technologies</li> <li>Post sensor data to cloud for IoT review</li> <li>Control devices from cloud data sources</li> </ol> <p>The two days cover IoT sensors and actuators, wireless communication technologies, cloud computing for IoT, collecting and posting sensor data with the ESP8266 over MQTT or REST API, remote device control, and IoT data analytics, visualization and security. An IoT kit is used throughout the training (the kit itself is not included in the course fee).</p> <h3>What it costs</h3> <p>The full fee is <strong>$750</strong> before GST. With WSQ funding:</p> <table> <tr><th>Who you are</th><th>Nett fee</th><th>Funding</th></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen / PR, aged 21 and above</td><td><strong>$442.50</strong></td><td>50% course-fee funding</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored</td><td><strong>$292.50</strong></td><td>70% course-fee funding</td></tr> </table> <p>GST of $67.50 (9%) applies to the full course fee. Funding is valid <strong>from 20 May 2020 to 19 May 2028</strong>. Eligible Singapore Citizens can apply <strong>SkillsFuture Credit</strong> and <strong>PSEA</strong> against the nett fee, NTUC members may claim <strong>UTAP</strong>, and employers can tap <strong>SFEC</strong> with <strong>Absentee Payroll</strong> support. Completion &mdash; at least 75% attendance plus a pass in the assessment &mdash; earns a Certificate of Achievement from Tertiary Infotech Academy and an <strong>OpenCert (Statement of Achievement) from SkillsFuture Singapore</strong>.</p> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-internet-of-things-iot-fundamental-for-beginners.html"><strong>Check the next class date and funding eligibility &rarr;</strong></a></p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is the course still hands-on like the 2017 class?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. An IoT kit is used throughout the training &mdash; you wire sensors, transmit real data over Wi-Fi and control real devices from the cloud, rather than watching a demonstration. Note the course fee does not include the kit itself.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need a programming or electronics background?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. The course is at Beginner level &mdash; the entry requirement is basic computer literacy and the ability to operate a laptop. The 2017 class was full of first-timers, and that is still who the course is designed for.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What is the ESP8266 and why does it still matter?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>A low-cost Wi-Fi microcontroller that made IoT accessible to beginners when it appeared in the mid-2010s. It is still on the course outline because it remains one of the cheapest, clearest ways to learn the pattern every IoT system uses: sense, transmit, store, act.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Was the 2017 class WSQ funded?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. WSQ funding for this course began on 20 May 2020. In 2017 learners paid the full fee &mdash; today, eligible Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above or SME-sponsored staff pay $292.50 nett for the same fundamentals, taught over two full days.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Where is the class held now?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>At 12 Woodlands Square, #07-85/86/87 Woods Square Tower 1, Singapore 737715 &mdash; a five-minute walk from Woodlands MRT (NS9). The venue is disabled-friendly. The Bukit Timah Shopping Centre premises in the photograph are from our early years.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>Where to go next</h2> <p>Nearly a decade on, the subject that needed explaining in 2017 now runs your building''s aircon, your carpark''s sensors and a good part of Singapore''s smart-nation infrastructure. The way in has not changed: one chip, one sensor, one afternoon.</p> <ol> <li>Join the next <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-internet-of-things-iot-fundamental-for-beginners.html"><strong>WSQ - Internet of Things (IoT) Fundamental for Beginners</strong></a> class &mdash; from <strong>$292.50 nett</strong> for eligible Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above.</li> <li>Then see how far the fundamentals stretch: <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/iotflow-self-hosted-iot-platform-python-sdk">we built our own self-hosted IoT platform with a Python SDK</a> on exactly the sense-transmit-store-act pattern taught in class.</li> </ol>', 'Tertiary Infotech Academy', 1, '2017-04-07', 'TGS-2020504020', 'TGS-2020504020', 'Our 2017 IoT Class at Bukit Timah | WSQ IoT Course Singapore', 'A look back at our 7 April 2017 Internet of Things class with Mr Deepak at Bukit Timah Shopping Centre, and how the course became today''s WSQ IoT Fundamental for Beginners. Funded from $292.50 nett.', 'iot class singapore 2017, internet of things course singapore, wsq iot course, esp8266 training, iot for beginners, mqtt course, skillsfuture iot, bukit timah shopping centre, iot fundamentals', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW() FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg > 0 AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT `post_id` FROM `mmd_blog_post` WHERE `url_key` = 'iot-class-2017-deepak-bukit-timah') x);

